# OpenLance web — environment. See also ../.env.example (shared root file).
#
# The web app reads almost nothing from the environment: it is a client-side UI
# that talks to the API service over HTTP. One variable matters, and it is
# baked in at BUILD time, not start time — changing it requires a rebuild.
#
# Runtime note: `pnpm install` symlinks the repo-root `.env.local` here, so this
# file is reference documentation, not something you copy per-package.

# ── API origin ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where apps/api lives, as the browser (and the proxy) reach it. Decided in one
# place, lib/api-base.ts. Three cases:
#
#   Separate origin  NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://localhost:4000
#     `pnpm dev`, Caddy / self-hosted, CI. The browser calls the API directly and
#     cross-origin, so the API must allow this origin (it reads APP_URI for that)
#     and, in production, share a cookie domain (COOKIE_DOMAIN on the API).
#     This is the default when the variable is unset.
#
#   Same origin      NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=          (set, but EMPTY)
#     `/api/*` is served beside the pages, so every request is the relative
#     `/api/...` — the Vercel services deployment (one domain, the root
#     vercel.json rewrites `/api/(.*)` to the api service). No CORS, no
#     COOKIE_DOMAIN: the gate cookies are host-only. The proxy's server-side job
#     lookup relies on that same rewrite, so this mode is for Vercel; elsewhere,
#     set the API's own URL.
#     For `vercel dev`, run `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE= vercel dev` (shell, not an empty
#     value in .env.local, which env tooling may drop): the Vercel default below is
#     keyed on the VERCEL variable, which that command may not pass to `next dev`.
#
#   On Vercel: leave it UNSET. next.config.ts defaults it to the empty string when
#     it builds there, so localhost:4000 can never be baked into the production
#     bundle. Do NOT point it at a separate API project, and delete any value left
#     over from an old two-project setup — it would silently put every call back
#     on a second origin. An explicit value (even an empty one) always wins.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix = inlined into the client bundle at build time. Never put
# a secret behind it; anything here is readable by every visitor.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://localhost:4000

# ── Where the values come from ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# APP_URI, API_URI, PORT, COOKIE_DOMAIN, DATABASE_URL, the chain variables and
# the relayer key are all read by the API service, not here — they belong in
# the root .env.local. See ../.env.example for the full annotated list.
